\begin{abstract}
We present a unified theoretical and computational framework that integrates real-time astrophysical observational telemetry into conservative gradient force field generators for spacecraft station-keeping in multi-body gravitational regimes. Focusing on the Sun-Earth L$_2$ Lagrange point neighborhood, we construct an engineered scalar potential expanded via spherical harmonics, where time-dependent amplitude coefficients are modulated through key-hashed projections of pulsar timing residuals and astrometric ephemerides. By enforcing the irrotational condition $\nabla \times (-\nabla \Phi) \equiv \mathbf{0}$, we guarantee that total mechanical energy is conserved and work remains path-independent across closed spatial loops. The control potential Hessian is integrated directly into the restricted three-body effective Jacobian matrix, enabling real-time eigenvalue diagnostics of the stability manifold. Through stochastic Monte Carlo simulations incorporating Markovian telemetry dropout models, we demonstrate robust orbital containment and survival probabilities exceeding mission-grade thresholds under severe solar radiation pressure and communication attenuation. This framework establishes a rigorous physical and cryptographic primitive for fuel-optimized, secure deep-space navigation architectures.
\end{abstract}

