\section{Stochastic Derivation of the Telemetry Dropout Transition Matrix}
To model the operational degradation of the cryptographic telemetry stream $D(t)$ under deep-space communication constraints, we employ a continuous-time Markov process. Let $S(t) \in \{S_0, S_1\}$ denote the binary link state governed by the master equation:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt} \begin{pmatrix} P(S_0, t) \\ P(S_1, t) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -\lambda & \mu \\ \lambda & -\mu \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} P(S_0, t) \\ P(S_1, t) \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}

\section{Explicit Form of the Spherical Harmonic Hessian Matrix}
The spatial components of the control potential Hessian $H_{\Phi}$ in Cartesian coordinates are computed via second-order central differences:
\begin{equation}
H_{ij}(\Phi) = \frac{\partial^2 \Phi}{\partial x_i \partial x_j} = \lim_{\epsilon \to 0} \frac{\Phi(x + \mathbf{e}_i \epsilon + \mathbf{e}_j \epsilon) - \Phi(x + \mathbf{e}_i \epsilon - \mathbf{e}_j \epsilon) - \Phi(x - \mathbf{e}_i \epsilon + \mathbf{e}_j \epsilon) + \Phi(x - \mathbf{e}_i \epsilon - \mathbf{e}_j \epsilon)}{4 \epsilon^2}
\end{equation}

